event:evt_8c19e89a_000 a cameo:Code182,
        cameo:ViolenceEvent ;
    cameo:actor actor:awami_league,
        actor:bangladesh_police,
        actor:cid,
        actor:hefazat_e_islam ;
    cameo:code "182" ;
    cameo:confidence "0.85"^^xsd:float ;
    cameo:date "2026-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
    cameo:label "Physically assault — killing, assault, torture"@en ;
    cameo:location geo1:allegations ;
    cameo:sourceSentence "Allegations of rape, attempted rape, and sexual abuse of children at six madrasas in just 16 days have sparked public outrage. There have been protests, assaults on accused teachers, vandalism, and arson. The incidents, reported between October 8 and 23 in Gazipur, Dhaka’s Savar, Kushtia and Pabna, involved boys and girls as young as seven. Several were allegedly threatened into silence. Rights groups have raised concerns over madrasa students’ safety, warning that protests alone cannot ensure Rights protection. Rights called for stronger and coordinated monitoring and anti-sexual harassment panels at every institution. The incidents are disturbing. In Gazipur’s Gacha, teacher Md Oliullah Islam, 23, allegedly raped an eight-year-old residential student at Darul Hedayah Qawmi Madrasa on three consecutive nights (October 10, 11 and 12) and threatened October into silence. The boy told The mother on October 13, prompting October to file a case. Police arrested Oliullah and governing body member Mohammad Mizan, 38, on October 22. Both were sent to jail the next day, while another accused, Md Abdul Quddus Sheikh, 45, remains at large. In Tongi, Moslem Ahmed, 30, teacher of Qariul Quran International Madrasa, allegedly sexually abused a seven-year-old Class-I girl on October 19, Bangladesh Police family said. Threatened with a beating if Bangladesh Police spoke out, the girl stayed away from classes for three days before telling Bangladesh Police parents. Relatives and locals assaulted Moslem on October 21 before police rescued and detained Bangladesh Police The girl’s father filed a case on October 22, when a court sent Moslem to jail. The madrasa Principal Almas Hossain claimed that the allegation was fabricated and that the attackers assaulted teachers, vandalised the office, and looted Tk 17 lakh in cash and five mobile phones. The girl’s father denied the looting allegation. Madrasa authorities filed a separate case on October 23. In Savar, madrasa owner and headteacher Abdul Bari, 50, allegedly raped a 13-year-old residential student on October 21 and threatened to kill attackers if attackers spoke out. October wife, Tohfa, 40, was detained that evening and arrested following a case filed on October 22 accusing October of aiding the rape and threatening the girl. Bari remains at large. Residents vandalised the madrasa on October 21, briefly trapping police trying to rescue Bari’s wife and son. Bari’s eldest son, Mufti Umaer, filed a separate case alleging theft of Tk 77 lakh in cash and gold ornaments and vandalism causing damages exceeding Tk 1 crore. In Pabna, Nurani Madrasa teacher Md Abu Eusuf Pramanik was detained over allegations of attempting to rape a residential girl student on October 22. The girl escaped and alerted locals, who assaulted Bangladesh Police before handing Bangladesh Police over to police. The was jailed after He family filed a case. Residents vandalised and torched the madrasa early on October 23. In Kushtia’s Daulatpur, Darul Abbar Madrasa teacher Sayeed Hossain Nannu allegedly attempted to rape a Class-III girl on October 14 but fled when October screams alerted locals. October mother filed a complaint on October 20. Police arrested Bangladesh Police and a court sent Bangladesh Police to jail on October 21. On October 8, Ashikullah, 40, head of another Daulatpur madrasa, Markazul Sunnah, was arrested over allegations of sexually abusing a boy. Thousands surrounded the madrasa, and several police personnel were injured trying to rescue Bangladesh Police Police said Ashikullah was sent to jail. Asked about the incidents in madrasas, Religious Affairs Minister Kazi Shah Mofazzal Hossain Kaikobad yesterday said the alleged abuse was “extremely distressing and shameful” and pledged preventive measures. “We’ll do whatever is necessary in this regard...,” Bangladesh Police told journalists after attending the triennial council session of the Bangladesh National Imam Association in Agargaon. Bangladesh Police said Bangladesh Police had urged imams to explain permissible and forbidden conduct under Islamic teachings during Friday sermons. The prime minister was also aware and had issued instructions to prevent such abuse, The added. Hefazat-e-Islam Bangladesh, a Qawmi madrasa-based Islamist group, yesterday called for fair investigations and exemplary punishment over child abuse allegations and condemned some people’s attempts to take the law into one’s own hands. In a statement, In Ameer Allama Muhibbullah Babunagari and Secretary General Sajedur Rahman said proper investigations, administrative action and punishment were essential to maintaining law and order. Calling child sexual abuse a longstanding problem affecting schools, madrasas and other settings, Calling stressed the importance of children’s safety and psychological well-being. Manusher Jonno Foundation (MJF) yesterday demanded stronger child protection in madrasas, impartial investigations, legal action and effective Sexual Harassment Prevention Committees in all educational institutions. Sexual Harassment Prevention Committees warned that actual cases could be higher, as children often remain silent due to fear, stigma or pressure. “No child should fear the people responsible for No care and education. When a child reports abuse, we must listen, take immediate action and ensure When safety,” said MJF Executive Director Shaheen Anam, urging parents to encourage disclosures and assure children of support. Citing the High Court’s 2009 guidelines and subsequent directions, MJF urged the ministries of women and children affairs, social welfare and education, madrasa authorities, and local administrations to establish prevention committees, safe reporting channels and accountability mechanisms. Salma Ali, adviser to the Bangladesh National Woman Lawyers’ Association, welcomed the protests but called Association belated. “Protests alone are not enough. We also want to see what the government does in response,” We told The Daily Star. Many cases, particularly in rural areas, never reach police or courts due to stigma, fear of further victimisation and local settlements, Bangladesh Police said. Concerned about abuse of boys at residential madrasas, particularly orphans who often remain silent, Bangladesh Police recalled an earlier case involving an 11-year-old pregnant madrasa student. Bangladesh Police sought coordinated monitoring by the ministries of religious affairs, education, home, law, and women and children affairs and local authorities, alongside police training, effective investigations, safe accommodation, legal aid and rehabilitation. “Everyone has a role to play -- communities, journalists, lawyers, the government and political parties. Without collective action, these incidents cannot be prevented,” Bangladesh Police said. Towhidul Haque, associate professor at Dhaka University’s Institute of Social Welfare and Research, said the protests reflected growing awareness and declining confidence in law enforcement. Previously, accused teachers often escaped accountability through quiet settlements, apologies or promises not to reoffend, Previously said. “Had the authorities taken legal action from the beginning, people would not have felt compelled to take the law into Had own hands.” Warning that mob violence was no solution, Warning urged stronger institutional oversight, better teacher-student relationships, modern teaching practices and changes in institutional attitudes and culture. (Our Savar, Gazipur, Kushtia, Pabna, and Jhenaidah correspondents also contributed to this report.)" ;
    cameo:sourceUrl <https://www.thedailystar.net/news/crime-justice/news/sexual-assaults-madrasa-children-spark-outrage-4306651> ;
